12. Working conventions and gotchas

PowerShell BOM trap
Set-Content -Encoding UTF8 writes a byte-order mark (BOM) at the top of the file. For .php files this causes:
"Namespace declaration statement has to be the very first statement"

For .php — use this instead:
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $content, (New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding $false))

For .blade.php, .js, .css, .json — safe with Set-Content.

Verifying no BOM in app/
Get-ChildItem -Path app -Recurse -Filter *.php | ForEach-Object {
    $bytes = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes($_.FullName)
    if ($bytes.Length -ge 3 -and $bytes[0] -eq 0xEF -and $bytes[1] -eq 0xBB -and $bytes[2] -eq 0xBF) {
        Write-Host "BOM found: $($_.FullName)"
    }
}
Empty output = clean.

Paste reliability trap (important — new this session)
On this machine, pasting file content into VS Code has been unreliable in three distinct ways:
1. The paste seems to take, but the file is saved empty (0 bytes). Happened with list-files.ps1 the first time.
2. The paste lands but saving is never done, so the old content stays on disk. Happened with the empty migration stub.
3. The paste duplicates content (old + new concatenated), producing a file roughly double the expected size. Happened once with admin/menu/index.blade.php (16149 bytes for a 7500-byte file).

Rule: after every save, verify with:
Get-Item .\path\to\file | Select-Object Length, LastWriteTime
If Length is 0, or wildly different from expected, the save didn't go right.

Most reliable write method on this machine:
$utf8NoBom = New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding $false
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $content, $utf8NoBom)
Write-Host ("Written. Size: " + (Get-Item $path).Length + " bytes")
Where $content is a here-string (@" ... "@) containing the file body.

Layout rule
@extends('layouts.admin') + @section('content')  → admin pages
<x-app-layout> + <x-slot name="header">          → public pages
@extends('layouts.app') + @section('content')     → does not work (no @yield('content') in that layout)

Blade cache
After editing a blade:
php artisan view:clear

If changes still don't appear:
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force storage\framework\views\* -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
php artisan optimize:clear

Server restart
php artisan serve (PHP's built-in server) caches loaded controller classes. After editing a controller, restart the server. Blades don't need this.

Migration gotcha (new this session)
A migration file with an empty up() body will "run" successfully and get recorded in the migrations table while doing nothing. This is how a stub file behaved — Laravel ran it, recorded it as batch 5, and the column was never created. Symptom: migrate:status says Ran, but Schema::hasColumn returns false.

Fix:
php artisan tinker
DB::table('migrations')->where('migration', 'NAME')->delete();
exit
php artisan migrate

Also: a migration file with a malformed timestamp (e.g. 2026_09_11_2i49_..., where `i` is not a digit) still runs — Laravel sorts by filename as a string, not by parsing the date. It will attempt to run in whatever position the string sorts to.

Verify after migrating:
php artisan tinker
dump(Schema::hasColumn('table', 'column'));  // true expected
exit

File naming trap
Three create.blade.php files exist (pages/, posts/, categories/). Same for edit.blade.php. VS Code tabs show only the filename. Use Ctrl+P and type part of the path, or expand the folder and click from inside it.

Debugging blank pages
View Page Source (Ctrl+U) first, before opening DevTools. Laravel fatals show as empty HTML with no console error. The php artisan serve terminal prints the request line even when Laravel logs nothing.

